{"name":"Rise and Shine","hex":"#ffe99e","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rise and shine","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Used to wish someone happiness upon waking up"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A traditional call for the number twenty-nine."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rise_and_shine"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rise and Shine (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Rise and Shine is a 1941 American comedy crime film directed by Allan Dwan and starring Jack Oakie, George Murphy and Linda Darnell. It was produced and distributed by Twentieth Century Fox. It is inspired by the 1933 autobiography My Life and Hard Times by James Thurber."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/5e/Rise_and_Shine_%28film%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rise_and_Shine_(film)"},{"title":"Rise and Shine (Randy Travis album)","blocks":[{"text":"Rise and Shine is the fourteenth studio album by American country music artist Randy Travis. It was released on October 15, 2002, by Word Records. It produced the single \"Three Wooden Crosses\", which in 2003 became his first Number One single on the Hot Country Singles & Tracks chart since 1994's \"Whisper My Name\". The only other single release from this album was \"Pray for the Fish\", which peaked at No. 48."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/0/09/Riseandshine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rise_and_Shine_(Randy_Travis_album)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/5e/Rise_and_Shine_%28film%29.jpg/250px-Rise_and_Shine_%28film%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/5e/Rise_and_Shine_%28film%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscale